{"causes": ["The political fragmentation of Poland into numerous duchies following the death of Bolesław V the Chaste in 1279 created a power vacuum and a desire for reunification.", "The expansionist ambitions of the Bohemian Premyslid dynasty under Wenceslaus II threatened Polish sovereignty, necessitating a strong ecclesiastical counter-force.", "The emerging sense of a distinct Polish national identity required institutional support, which the Primate of Poland was uniquely positioned to provide."], "effects": ["Jakub Świnka's actions significantly contributed to the eventual reunification of the Polish Kingdom under Władysław I Łokietek.", "His promotion of the Polish language in religious and educational contexts strengthened national identity and cultural cohesion.", "He solidified the role of the Archbishop of Gniezno as a key political player and defender of Polish statehood against foreign influence."], "relationships": [{"sourceSlug": "jakub-winka", "sourceName": "Jakub Świnka", "verb": "COLLABORATES_WITH", "targetSlug": "wladyslaw-i-lokietek", "targetName": "Władysław I the Elbow-high", "context": "Jakub Świnka actively supported Władysław I Łokietek's claim to the Polish throne against foreign rulers from 1296 until his own death in 1314."}, {"sourceSlug": "synod-of-leczyca-1285", "sourceName": "Synod of Łęczyca (1285)", "verb": "OCCURS_DURING", "targetSlug": "jakub-winka", "targetName": "Jakub Świnka", "context": "The Synod of Łęczyca, which promoted the Polish language, was convened and presided over by Archbishop Jakub Świnka in 1285."}, {"sourceSlug": "jakub-winka", "sourceName": "Jakub Świnka", "verb": "PARTICIPATES_IN", "targetSlug": "reunification-of-poland", "targetName": "Reunification of Poland", "context": "Jakub Świnka was a central ecclesiastical figure in the political efforts to reunify the fragmented Polish duchies into a single kingdom in the late 13th and early 14th centuries."}, {"sourceSlug": "jakub-winka", "sourceName": "Jakub Świnka", "verb": "OCCURS_IN", "targetSlug": "gniezno-poland", "targetName": "Gniezno, Poland", "context": "Jakub Świnka served as the Archbishop of Gniezno, the ecclesiastical capital of Poland, from 1283 to 1314."}, {"sourceSlug": "wenceslaus-ii-of-bohemia", "sourceName": "Wenceslaus II of Bohemia", "verb": "INFLUENCES", "targetSlug": "jakub-winka", "targetName": "Jakub Świnka", "context": "Wenceslaus II's attempts to assert Bohemian rule over Poland provoked strong opposition from Jakub Świnka, who excommunicated him in 1300."}], "places": [{"name": "Gniezno, Poland", "role": "Archbishopric See"}, {"name": "Łęczyca, Poland", "role": "Synod Location"}, {"name": "Kraków, Poland", "role": "Royal Capital"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: Jakub Świnka", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "jakub-świnka"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q322448", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jakub_%C5%9Awinka"], "tags": ["Poland", "Religious Figures", "Europe", "comparative-religion", "doctrine-development", "cultural-diffusion"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "Jakub Świnka is remembered as a landmark person of the Medieval era, referenced in 0 Wikipedia language editions. Key lasting impacts include: Shaped religious practice and spiritual communities; Contributed to synthesis of classical and religious thought informing Renaissance and Reformati.", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 7, "significanceNarrative": "Jakub Świnka was a crucial figure in the reunification of the Polish Kingdom, laying the ecclesiastical and ideological groundwork that enabled Władysław I's coronation and the re-establishment of a strong Polish state. His efforts to promote the Polish language also had lasting cultural impact, reinforcing national identity for centuries.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-11-12T08:20:58Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#43181064646", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Jakub Świnka was a Polish Catholic priest, the Archbishop of Gniezno and a notable politician and statesman, supporter of the idea of unification of all Polish lands under the rule of Władysław I the Elbow-high. His coat of arms was Świnka.", "newValue": "Jakub Świnka, Archbishop of Gniezno from 1283 to 1314, emerged as a pivotal ecclesiastical and political figure in late 13th and early 14th-century Poland, actively championing the restoration of the unified Polish Kingdom after its fragmentation into duchies. His tenure coincided with a critical juncture following the death of Przemysł II in 1296 and the subsequent struggle for the Polish crown between Wenceslaus II of Bohemia and Władysław I the Elbow-high. Świnka strategically leveraged the authority of the Polish Church, particularly its primate's role, to oppose foreign domination and foster a strong sense of national identity.

His most significant actions included presiding over synods, notably the Synod of Łęczyca in 1285, which mandated the use of the Polish language in sermons and school instruction, a powerful cultural assertion against Germanization. He consistently supported Władysław I Łokietek (the Elbow-high) against the Premyslid dynasty's claims to the Polish throne, excommunicating Wenceslaus II in 1300 and his successor Wenceslaus III in 1305, thereby delegitimizing their rule in the eyes of the Polish populace and the Church. Świnka's diplomatic efforts also extended to securing papal support for Władysław's coronation.

Świnka's unwavering commitment to Polish sovereignty and his instrumental role in Władysław I's eventual coronation in 1320 (after Świnka's death, but as a direct result of the political groundwork he laid) profoundly shaped the trajectory of the Polish state. His policies helped consolidate the Polish Church's national character, distinguishing it from German ecclesiastical influence, and provided a crucial ideological and practical foundation for the re-establishment of the kingdom, which would endure for centuries. The emphasis on the Polish language also contributed to the long-term cultural cohesion of the nascent nation."}, {"timestamp": "2026-11-12T08:20:58Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#43181064646", "field": "detailsJson.causes", "oldValue": [{"title": "Educated at in the 500 – 1500 CE", "type": "Person", "year": "500"}, {"title": "Influenced by in the 500 – 1500 CE", "type": "Person", "year": "500"}, {"title": "Born into in the 500 – 1500 CE", "type": "Person", "year": "500"}], "newValue": ["The political fragmentation of Poland into numerous duchies following the death of Bolesław V the Chaste in 1279 created a power vacuum and a desire for reunification.", "The expansionist ambitions of the Bohemian Premyslid dynasty under Wenceslaus II threatened Polish sovereignty, necessitating a strong ecclesiastical counter-force.", "The emerging sense of a distinct Polish national identity required institutional support, which the Primate of Poland was uniquely positioned to provide."]}, {"timestamp": "2026-11-12T08:20:58Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#43181064646", "field": "detailsJson.effects", "oldValue": [{"title": "Founded through Jakub Świnka's historical significance", "type": "Person"}, {"title": "Wrote through Jakub Świnka's historical significance", "type": "Person"}, {"title": "Established through Jakub Świnka's historical significance", "type": "Person"}, {"title": "Transformed through Jakub Świnka's historical significance", "typ